okay picture a moba like dota or AoS and then subtract the heroes and the jungle
and the items and what's left? A bunch of units walking toward one another, and
fighting in the middle, barely moving the frontlines at all. That's not ideal,
so let's make it a roguelike, and let two teams share the upgrades they gather
to create powerful waves that overcome the enemy.

How are the upgrades gathered?

How are the upgrades shared?

How are the upgrades more interesting than heroes?

good points. Let's say that whenever a wave is fully defeated, an upgrade goes
into the pool for a team. It's randomly selected. It's just a big treasure chest
that has all the upgrades you've gathered. They can be dynamically re-assigned
to each lane, but if a player wants to they can lock an upgrade in place so that
their allies don't move it around - they've got a thing going, y'see. The allies
can "ping" an upgrade to ask it to be unlocked, and if both of them do so then
it automatically unlocks. Every once in a while, there's a "siege-surge" which
is a silly name but whatever, and in this mode there's a continuous stream of
units emitted from both bases instead of waves, and all the upgrades get put
back into the pool. The players have to quickly re-assign them to re-create the
strategies they were using, or create new ones quickly to throw off their foes.
During a siege-surge, a player can't place an upgrade in the same lane twice in
a row, to help ensure they don't just take ownership over a lane and go blind to
what's happening in the others. At the end of the siege-surge, of which there
are three over the course of a full game, the players gain a powerful boon that
helps them overcome a challenge that appears. After the challenge, both teams
get to keep their boon. This upgrade is applied to all three lanes at once. The
challenge is a large, powerful monster that appears in the center lane, two of
them, and each marches toward the opposite team's base. If they don't defeat it
in time, well, game over, but the good news is all three lanes spawn their units
in the center lane until the challenge is defeated.

There's also a resource gained when units are slain that is personal to each
player - these resources are used to spawn hero units that fight until they die.
Each player selects a character to play as, their commander, and each one will
have a different resource, like gold or mana. Some commanders might share a
resource but that's just visual flair, it's mechanically the same thing. These
resources are used to spawn hero units, which are strong powerful units that are
chosen to walk down a lane and fight enemies. However at the junctions, when the
path turns, they can be told to switch lanes. The player can configure this by
clicking a sign-post in the world that points where the heroes will be routed to
when they walk past. These hero units are unique to each commander character,
and there's at least three per commander, ideally five. They behave like regular
units so we better make sure that our unit AI is top notch.

Each path has guard-towers, which deal damage to enemies and spawn guards that
walk around in the area around the tower. If the tower is destroyed, the team
that destroyed it gets three unit upgrades as a reward. There's two towers per
lane, and three more in the base itself. The guards in the base will move to
attack any invaders no matter which lane they came from, but the range on their
arrows is such that they probably will only be able to hit the units that came
from a single lane - it's just a radius around them.

players are allowed to put all their upgrades in one lane. This is fine.

There is a central building called a "library" and if it is destroyed, then the
team that did so wins the game. There is one library per base, and it is placed
behind the guard towers. It has about as much health as 1.5 guard towers. Each
guard tower is the same strength, and some upgrades can be applied to the guard
towers instead of the wave units. To do so, they slot them into one of the
towers in the lane, and then all of the guard towers in that lane receive the
effect of that upgrade. If an upgrade is slotted into a guard tower, then it
also affects the three towers in the base - which means if the left flank is
faltering enough that the enemy is in your base, and you have upgrades in the
center and right guard towers, those upgrades will affect the guard tower that
is covering the left side of the base as well as the center lane (for the
upgrades slotted into the center lane's towers) and the right lane (for the
upgrades slotted into the right lane's towers). However, upgrades cannot be
slotted into the guard towers in the base itself - instead, you can slot them
into the library, which will apply them to the base's guard towers only. This is
rare and usually only occurs if all the lane towers are destroyed. Not ideal!

Hero units are about as powerful as 2.5 wave units, but they have abilities
while the wave units do not. A player can select a wave to spawn a unit on, and
it will spawn immediately. Hero units can be spawned on any guard tower, unless
enemy units are within a certain radius - then they have to be spawned one tower
back. Units can also be spawned on the library, in which case they will pick the
lane to move toward that has enemy units that are closest to the library - not
in terms of distance as-the-crow-flies, but rather in terms of milestones thru
the map - "this lane is past the first guard tower" is closer than "this lane
we've pushed into their base" of course.
